<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melisende, Queen of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1105–<a href="/years/1161">1161</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Queen Melisende</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melisende was Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1131 to 1153, the eldest daughter of Baldwin II. She ruled jointly with her husband Fulk of Anjou and, after his death in 1143, with her son Baldwin III, until he forced her from power in 1153. A patron of churches and the arts, she is associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melisende-psalter">Melisende Psalter</a> and the crusader rebuilding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melisende-queen-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Melisende, Queen of Jerusalem" width="317" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronation_of_Melisende.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Melisende was the eldest daughter of Baldwin II, then Frankish count of Edessa, and Morphia of Melitene, an Armenian noblewoman of the Greek Orthodox faith. She and her sisters grew up speaking Armenian and probably Greek, and became proficient in French. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> died in 1118, her father was elected to succeed him, and the family joined him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in late 1119. Melisende remained unmarried for an unusually long time, since her father may still have hoped for a son. After Morphia died, probably in 1126 or 1127, Baldwin named Melisende his heir, and in 1129 she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">Fulk V of Anjou</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Baldwin II died on 21 August 1131, having commended the kingdom to Melisende, Fulk and their infant son Baldwin, and the patriarch crowned Fulk and Melisende on 14 September. For the first five years Fulk did not associate her with his public acts, against both their marriage contract and her father's will. In 1134 noblemen led by Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-ii-of-jaffa">Hugh II of Jaffa</a> revolted against Fulk. After their reconciliation, Fulk never again issued a charter for the kingdom without her consent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melisende-psalter">Melisende Psalter</a>, a luxurious prayer book, is believed to have been commissioned by Fulk for her around 1135.</p><p>Fulk died on 10 November 1143 after a fall from his horse. Melisende took full charge of the government and was crowned again on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1143 together with her son Baldwin III, then thirteen. She appointed her cousin Manasses of Hierges constable, to command the army in her name. After the fall of Edessa in 1144 she urged intervention from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which led to the Second Crusade. Her relations with Baldwin broke down as she refused to give him real authority. Baldwin invaded her share of the divided kingdom and besieged her in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, and she agreed to retire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After her fall Melisende still advised her son and in 1157 took a military initiative while he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, when the cave-fortress of el-Hablis was recovered from the Muslims at her insistence. She died in 1161 after an illness, possibly a stroke. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, who took her side, praised her wisdom, but two disasters, the fall of Edessa and the failure to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1148, came in her reign, though her personal blame is arguable. The historian Folda calls her the foremost patron of art in the twelfth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melisende of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The crown passed several times through women: <b>Melisende</b>, daughter of Baldwin II, ruled with her husband Fulk of Anjou from 1131 and remained powerful after his death, and the claims of Sibylla, Isabella I and Isabella II brought in husbands from the West.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After besieging <b>Queen Melisende</b> in the Tower of David, Baldwin III had become master of Jerusalem by this date, and she retired to Nablus under a negotiated settlement.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Şanlıurfa Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.25,39.04&amp;year=1141" aria-label="Şanlıurfa Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Melisende, Queen of Jerusalem"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5613 4357h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5613 4357h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.25,39.04&amp;year=1141">What was here in 1141? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
